# Vitryna Publish setup

Generated for `{{ .SourceRepository }}` → `{{ .GitHub.Repository }}` using `{{ .Pins.Version }}`.
This guide is explicitly private in `.vitryna.yml`. Review and commit all three generated files.

## Before enabling publication

1. Initialize the public target's `{{ .GitHub.BaseBranch }}` branch yourself. Vitryna never creates target history, changes protection, or pushes the public base.
2. Create a dedicated GitHub App installed only on the selected public target. Do not install this App on other repositories or give it bypass rights.
3. Grant Metadata read, Contents write, and Pull requests write. Add Workflows write when the public edition includes, replaces, or removes public workflows. Classic-protection auto mode additionally requires Administration read; manual and effective-ruleset modes do not.
4. In the source repository, set variable `VITRYNA_APP_CLIENT_ID` and secret `VITRYNA_APP_PRIVATE_KEY`. The workflow uses its own `GITHUB_TOKEN` for source/artifact reads. Never place credentials in `.vitryna.yml`.
5. Protect the trusted source branch; require review of `.vitryna.yml` and `.github/workflows/vitryna-publish.yml` (for example through CODEOWNERS). Changes to destination identity require workflow regeneration and review.
6. Protect the public base with required public CI and/or reviews. Enable Actions failure notifications. If reviews gate auto-merge, dismiss stale approvals or require approval after the last push.
7. Replace or review the project verification command in the `verify` job. Run builds into a directory outside the receipt-bound source tree; source byte/mode/inventory changes fail verification.

## Review your visibility rules

The requested public paths are exact files or entire subtrees. A public subtree intentionally includes future descendants. Every remaining currently tracked file is an exact private rule; new paths outside selected subtrees require a deliberate classification decision. `.vitryna.yml` and the Vitryna workflow are automatic private controls; this guide has an explicit private rule.

After committing, inspect the selected snapshot:

```sh
vitryna check --format json
vitryna explain README.md
vitryna export --output ../public-preview --receipt ../public-preview.receipt.json
```

The generated workflow has four jobs: invalidate → seal → verify → reconcile. Project commands run only against a disposable receipt-checked copy without target/App credentials. Publication re-downloads and revalidates the original immutable seal using its exact ID, digest, run, and source identity. Artifacts stay private for seven days. Preview diff never suppresses trusted no-diff reconciliation.

Private pull requests and manual dispatch with `publish: false` preview only. A dispatch requesting publication fails if its commit is no longer the trusted source tip. GitHub's queue order is not freshness evidence.

## Merge policy

Current mode: `{{ .GitHub.Merge.Mode }}`. Manual merge is the default. Explicit auto mode requests GitHub-native auto-merge only after checking a positive blocking gate, method availability, and review freshness; Vitryna never invokes an immediate merge endpoint or bypasses public protection.

An armed public PR can merge before a later private push's asynchronous invalidation succeeds. There is no atomic freshest-source merge guarantee. Do not repurpose the managed `{{ .GitHub.PromotionBranch }}` branch as a base or default branch; runtime guards cannot make concurrent administrator changes atomic.

## Credentials and recovery

Control jobs mint independent, short-lived exact-target installation tokens and revoke them on completion. Rotate the App key by replacing the source secret and retiring the old key; revoke/suspend the App if credentials are exposed. A failed invalidation can leave an older PR armed: inspect the failed job summary and close/disarm that exact owned candidate before retrying from the current trusted tip.

No-diff runs keep the owned candidate closed and lease-delete only its promotion ref. Unowned/ambiguous PRs, stale source, missing target base, changed default identity, artifact mismatches, or lease conflicts stop publication. Never force over a competing head or rewrite public default history as recovery.

